1
00:00:15,010 --> 00:00:15,900
What you're looking at

2
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:17,470
is a chain of circles.

3
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:19,240
Each one rides on the edge

4
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:20,510
of the one before,

5
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:22,710
spinning at its own steady speed.

6
00:00:23,690 --> 00:00:25,460
Follow the tip of the very last

7
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:27,740
circle, and it draws a treble clef.

8
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:30,670
Here's what I find strange about this.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,260
A circle is about the

10
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:33,490
roundest thing there is.

11
00:00:33,740 --> 00:00:36,020
No corners, no straight edges.

12
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:37,140
And yet the right circles

13
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:38,540
can draw sharp turns,

14
00:00:38,790 --> 00:00:41,140
even a square with four perfect corners.

15
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:42,940
So how does something that

16
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:45,030
only goes round draw a corner?

17
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:48,220
The answer is something called a Fourier

18
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:50,340
series, named after Joseph Fourier,

19
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:52,260
who found it about two hundred years ago

20
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:54,520
while studying, of all things, heat.

21
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,430
And it makes a bold claim.

22
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:58,600
Almost any loop you could draw can

23
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:00,510
be built out of spinning circles.

24
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:02,930
Think of a chord on a piano.

25
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:04,980
You hear one sound, but it's really

26
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:06,550
a few notes played together.

27
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:09,200
A Fourier series lets you take a shape

28
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:11,000
and pick out the circles inside it.

29
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:13,060
By the end, you and I will

30
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:14,440
have a recipe that finds the

31
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:15,890
circles for any drawing.

32
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:17,460
I'll put it on screen now,

33
00:01:17,460 --> 00:01:18,900
just so you'll recognize it later.

34
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:20,840
As with anything new,

35
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:22,210
it's best to start easy.

36
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:24,580
So let's begin with a single circle.

37
00:01:25,290 --> 00:01:26,520
Picture an arrow pinned at

38
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,220
the center, turning steadily.

39
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:30,160
For now, ignore everything

40
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:31,960
except how high its tip is.

41
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:32,820
Up,

42
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:34,090
down, up,

43
00:01:34,290 --> 00:01:34,730
down.

44
00:01:35,430 --> 00:01:37,150
Now let time move to the right,

45
00:01:37,410 --> 00:01:39,080
and let that height leave a trail.

46
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:41,580
What you get is a sine wave.

47
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:43,100
It's the simplest wave there

48
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:45,040
is, and really, it's just a

49
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:46,220
circle seen from the side.

50
00:01:46,890 --> 00:01:49,210
A spinning circle has only three settings.

51
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:50,460
There's its size.

52
00:01:50,670 --> 00:01:52,620
A bigger circle makes a taller wave.

53
00:01:53,310 --> 00:01:54,330
There's its speed.

54
00:01:54,530 --> 00:01:55,680
Spin twice as fast,

55
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:57,570
and the wave wiggles twice as often.

56
00:01:58,240 --> 00:01:59,810
And there's where it starts.

57
00:02:00,110 --> 00:02:02,080
Start the arrow a quarter turn ahead,

58
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:03,940
and the whole wave slides over.

59
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:06,760
Size, speed, and starting point.

60
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:08,950
That's everything one circle can do.

61
00:02:09,610 --> 00:02:11,200
There's another view I really like.

62
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:13,460
Slide the circle forward through time,

63
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:15,550
and its tip traces out a spring.

64
00:02:16,030 --> 00:02:17,160
From the side, the spring

65
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:18,280
looks like a sine wave.

66
00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:19,760
From above, it looks like

67
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:21,290
its twin, the cosine.

68
00:02:21,730 --> 00:02:23,870
Two shadows of one turning motion.

69
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:26,990
One circle gives you one smooth wave.

70
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:28,500
So let's add a second.

71
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:30,460
Put a smaller circle on the

72
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:31,340
tip of the first,

73
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:33,530
and let it spin three times as fast.

74
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:35,600
Now the tip rides on two motions

75
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:37,360
at once, so its height is the

76
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:39,030
first height plus the second.

77
00:02:39,790 --> 00:02:41,610
The wave picks up a little ripple.

78
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:43,990
Now let's be more deliberate.

79
00:02:44,370 --> 00:02:46,440
Make the first circle a little taller

80
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:47,770
than the wave we're aiming for.

81
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:49,960
The second spins three times as

82
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:51,980
fast, at one third the size.

83
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:53,030
The next,

84
00:02:53,210 --> 00:02:55,290
five times as fast, at one fifth.

85
00:02:55,690 --> 00:02:57,930
Then seven times, at one seventh.

86
00:02:59,110 --> 00:03:00,250
Before I keep going,

87
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:01,400
take a moment to guess.

88
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:03,260
What do you think this is turning into?

89
00:03:04,330 --> 00:03:05,800
Watch the top flatten out,

90
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:07,250
and the sides get steeper.

91
00:03:07,450 --> 00:03:09,290
Here it is with ten circles...

92
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:10,370
fifty...

93
00:03:10,970 --> 00:03:11,850
two hundred.

94
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:13,900
It's a square wave.

95
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:16,240
Flat on top, flat on the bottom,

96
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:17,910
with straight walls in between.

97
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:19,300
Smooth circles,

98
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:20,940
adding up to sharp corners.

99
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:23,110
But look closely at a corner.

100
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:24,880
Right before every jump,

101
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:26,160
there's a small spike.

102
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:28,320
Add more circles, and the spike gets

103
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:30,630
thinner, but it never gets any shorter.

104
00:03:30,630 --> 00:03:31,885
It stays at about nine

105
00:03:31,885 --> 00:03:32,980
percent of the jump.

106
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:35,560
This is called the Gibbs phenomenon,

107
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:36,790
and it's telling us something.

108
00:03:37,090 --> 00:03:39,170
Sharp corners are expensive.

109
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:41,540
Now you might be wondering why

110
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:42,820
anyone would build waves out of

111
00:03:42,820 --> 00:03:44,170
circles in the first place.

112
00:03:44,490 --> 00:03:46,180
The short answer is heat.

113
00:03:46,910 --> 00:03:48,020
In eighteen oh seven,

114
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:49,500
Fourier was trying to describe

115
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:51,230
how heat spreads through a solid.

116
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:52,700
Take a metal ring,

117
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:54,380
heat one half with a flame,

118
00:03:54,610 --> 00:03:55,580
cool the other half with

119
00:03:55,580 --> 00:03:56,970
ice, and let it go.

120
00:03:57,670 --> 00:03:59,840
Hot on one side, cold on the other.

121
00:04:00,220 --> 00:04:01,260
Unroll the ring,

122
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:02,730
and that's our square wave.

123
00:04:03,530 --> 00:04:05,200
Fourier's move was to break the

124
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:06,960
temperature into simple waves.

125
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:08,740
One that goes around the ring once,

126
00:04:08,910 --> 00:04:10,100
one that goes around three

127
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:12,140
times, then five, and so on.

128
00:04:12,830 --> 00:04:13,920
Each of those waves does

129
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:15,120
something very simple.

130
00:04:15,370 --> 00:04:17,850
It keeps its shape, and it just fades.

131
00:04:17,850 --> 00:04:19,310
And the wigglier the wave,

132
00:04:19,310 --> 00:04:20,560
the faster it fades.

133
00:04:20,890 --> 00:04:22,120
Three times the wiggles,

134
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:23,460
nine times as fast.

135
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:26,350
So the sharp edges melt away first,

136
00:04:26,540 --> 00:04:28,720
the ring settles into one gentle wave,

137
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:30,760
and then into an even warmth.

138
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:32,730
But for this to work,

139
00:04:32,730 --> 00:04:34,080
any starting temperature,

140
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:35,700
even one with sudden jumps,

141
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:38,050
had to be a sum of smooth waves.

142
00:04:38,050 --> 00:04:39,520
Some of the best mathematicians

143
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:41,100
of his day didn't buy it.

144
00:04:41,830 --> 00:04:43,950
Well, you and I just watched it happen.

145
00:04:44,750 --> 00:04:45,980
Now let's bring all this

146
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:47,000
back to our drawing.

147
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:48,940
So far, we've only watched

148
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:50,220
the height of the tip.

149
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:51,990
But it also moves left and right.

150
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:53,400
So let's use both,

151
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:54,460
and let the tip draw

152
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:55,440
right on the page.

153
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:58,360
This picture is actually very old.

154
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:00,420
Nearly two thousand years ago,

155
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:01,820
astronomers tracked the planets

156
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:03,970
with circles riding on circles.

157
00:05:03,970 --> 00:05:06,160
They were wrong about how the sky works.

158
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:08,130
But they were right about the circles.

159
00:05:08,130 --> 00:05:09,250
With enough of them,

160
00:05:09,250 --> 00:05:11,130
you can follow almost any path.

161
00:05:11,790 --> 00:05:14,090
On a page, direction matters.

162
00:05:14,090 --> 00:05:16,380
A circle can turn counterclockwise,

163
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:17,960
which we'll call a positive speed,

164
00:05:18,230 --> 00:05:20,090
or clockwise, a negative speed.

165
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:22,140
A circle with speed zero

166
00:05:22,140 --> 00:05:23,210
doesn't turn at all.

167
00:05:23,210 --> 00:05:25,170
It just sets where the drawing sits.

168
00:05:26,370 --> 00:05:27,500
Here's a friendly example

169
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:28,740
we'll keep coming back to.

170
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:31,730
Take a big circle turning forward once,

171
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:33,050
and add a small one,

172
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:34,380
one ninth the size,

173
00:05:34,380 --> 00:05:36,540
turning backward three times as fast.

174
00:05:37,790 --> 00:05:40,180
The tip traces out a rounded square.

175
00:05:41,060 --> 00:05:42,290
And the square from the beginning?

176
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:45,200
Its first two circles are exactly these.

177
00:05:46,230 --> 00:05:47,820
In general, we line up circles

178
00:05:47,820 --> 00:05:49,410
of every whole number speed.

179
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:51,760
Zero, one, minus one,

180
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:53,630
two, minus two, and so on.

181
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:56,120
Each has two settings left to choose,

182
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:58,180
its size and its starting angle.

183
00:05:59,230 --> 00:06:00,620
So here's the real question.

184
00:06:00,910 --> 00:06:02,380
If I hand you a drawing,

185
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:04,080
how do you find the size and

186
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:05,760
starting angle of every circle?

187
00:06:06,850 --> 00:06:08,720
Let's warm up with the easiest circle,

188
00:06:08,890 --> 00:06:09,850
speed zero.

189
00:06:10,790 --> 00:06:12,560
It never moves, so it should sit

190
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:13,880
at the center of the drawing.

191
00:06:14,170 --> 00:06:15,900
But what does center even mean,

192
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:17,470
for a squiggly shape like this?

193
00:06:18,350 --> 00:06:19,580
Here's a natural answer.

194
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:21,660
Spread dots along the path,

195
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:23,360
evenly in time, and average

196
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:24,380
all their positions.

197
00:06:24,790 --> 00:06:26,480
That average is the balance point

198
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:28,200
of the drawing, and it's exactly

199
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:30,010
where the speed zero circle sits.

200
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:32,640
That's because every other circle

201
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:34,130
spins all the way around,

202
00:06:34,130 --> 00:06:36,450
and a full turn averages out to nothing.

203
00:06:37,580 --> 00:06:39,190
That gives us the key idea.

204
00:06:39,560 --> 00:06:41,820
Things that spin average out to zero.

205
00:06:42,100 --> 00:06:43,870
Things that stand still don't.

206
00:06:44,750 --> 00:06:47,000
Now, the speed one circle spins,

207
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:48,330
so the average can't see it.

208
00:06:48,770 --> 00:06:50,620
Unless we can make it stand still.

209
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:52,540
Here's the trick.

210
00:06:52,540 --> 00:06:55,000
As the pen goes around, turn the whole

211
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,530
drawing backward, one full turn per loop.

212
00:06:58,000 --> 00:06:59,600
The speed one circle was turning

213
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:01,720
forward once, and now it's also

214
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:03,750
turned backward once, so it stops.

215
00:07:04,650 --> 00:07:06,630
Every other circle is still spinning,

216
00:07:06,630 --> 00:07:08,680
so when we average, they all wash out.

217
00:07:09,140 --> 00:07:10,800
What's left is the speed one

218
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:12,500
circle, frozen in place.

219
00:07:13,470 --> 00:07:15,580
As a sanity check, let's try our rounded

220
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:17,110
square, where we know the answer.

221
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:19,360
Turn it backward once and average,

222
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:20,800
and the balance point lands right

223
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:22,350
on the tip of the big circle.

224
00:07:23,060 --> 00:07:24,590
Turn it forward three times,

225
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:26,060
and it finds the small circle,

226
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:28,110
one ninth the size, just as it should.

227
00:07:28,910 --> 00:07:30,120
Try speed two,

228
00:07:30,120 --> 00:07:31,820
and the average falls to zero.

229
00:07:32,010 --> 00:07:33,260
There's no circle there.

230
00:07:34,170 --> 00:07:35,920
If you hold on to just one idea

231
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:37,860
from this video, make it this one.

232
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:40,720
To find any circle, spin the drawing

233
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:42,430
against it, and take the average.

234
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:43,800
Now we can read the line

235
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:44,680
from the beginning.

236
00:07:45,130 --> 00:07:46,500
C n equals the

237
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:47,380
integral from zero

238
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:49,260
to one, of f of t,

239
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:50,480
times e to the minus

240
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:52,864
two pi i n t, d t.

241
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:54,240
It looks dense,

242
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:56,110
but it's really just two steps.

243
00:07:56,570 --> 00:07:57,900
F of t is the pen's

244
00:07:57,900 --> 00:07:59,210
position at time t.

245
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:01,460
Multiplying by e to the minus

246
00:08:01,460 --> 00:08:03,960
two pi i n t turns the drawing

247
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:05,780
backward, n times per loop.

248
00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:07,660
If you saw our video on complex

249
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:09,180
numbers, that's just a point

250
00:08:09,180 --> 00:08:10,300
turning around a circle.

251
00:08:10,730 --> 00:08:12,820
Then the integral from zero to one

252
00:08:12,820 --> 00:08:15,050
takes the average over one trip around.

253
00:08:15,470 --> 00:08:17,100
Spin, then average.

254
00:08:17,270 --> 00:08:18,370
That's all it says.

255
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:20,370
So let's give it a real test.

256
00:08:20,750 --> 00:08:21,820
Here's our treble clef,

257
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:23,440
as one continuous line.

258
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:25,340
We place a thousand dots along

259
00:08:25,340 --> 00:08:26,980
it, and for every speed from

260
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:28,174
minus one hundred to one

261
00:08:28,174 --> 00:08:30,200
hundred, we spin and average.

262
00:08:30,630 --> 00:08:32,810
Out come two hundred and one circles.

263
00:08:33,240 --> 00:08:34,920
Sort them from biggest to smallest,

264
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:36,480
and chain them tip to tail.

265
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:38,770
With one circle, we get a loop.

266
00:08:39,050 --> 00:08:40,680
With five, a rough blob.

267
00:08:41,070 --> 00:08:42,990
By twenty, you can start to see it.

268
00:08:43,340 --> 00:08:44,540
And with two hundred,

269
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:45,600
the circles trace the

270
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:47,240
clef almost perfectly.

271
00:08:47,700 --> 00:08:49,840
Up close, even the tiniest circles

272
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:51,770
are adding detail the others can't.

273
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:53,460
Remember how I said sharp

274
00:08:53,460 --> 00:08:54,670
corners are expensive?

275
00:08:54,980 --> 00:08:56,160
Smooth curves come from

276
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:57,530
the first few circles.

277
00:08:57,750 --> 00:08:59,730
Sharp turns need all the rest.

278
00:09:00,140 --> 00:09:01,900
When the pen jumps, like in the square

279
00:09:01,900 --> 00:09:03,970
wave, the circles shrink slowly.

280
00:09:04,190 --> 00:09:04,980
One third,

281
00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:05,850
one fifth.

282
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:07,960
When the pen only turns a corner,

283
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:10,380
like our square, they shrink much faster.

284
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:12,361
One ninth, one twenty fifth.

285
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:13,990
The smoother the shape,

286
00:09:13,990 --> 00:09:15,590
the fewer circles it takes.

287
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,300
That's why this idea shows up everywhere.

288
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:20,060
Think back to the piano chord.

289
00:09:20,060 --> 00:09:21,770
A sound is a wave,

290
00:09:21,770 --> 00:09:23,100
so it has circles too.

291
00:09:23,270 --> 00:09:25,040
A singer and a violin playing

292
00:09:25,040 --> 00:09:26,960
the same note spin their slowest

293
00:09:26,960 --> 00:09:28,640
circle at the same speed.

294
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:30,500
It's the faster circles on top

295
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:31,720
that make them sound different.

296
00:09:32,100 --> 00:09:33,820
And your phone uses a cousin of this

297
00:09:33,820 --> 00:09:35,980
idea to shrink photos and music,

298
00:09:35,980 --> 00:09:37,640
keeping the big circles and dropping

299
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:39,330
the tiny ones you won't notice.

300
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:40,820
So let's step back and

301
00:09:40,820 --> 00:09:41,920
look at what we've built.

302
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:43,560
One spinning circle gives you

303
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:45,800
the simplest wave, with a size,

304
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:47,470
a speed, and a starting point.

305
00:09:47,830 --> 00:09:49,420
Stack circles tip to tail,

306
00:09:49,420 --> 00:09:50,950
and their motions add up.

307
00:09:50,950 --> 00:09:52,970
With the right sizes and speeds,

308
00:09:52,970 --> 00:09:55,400
smooth circles can build sharp corners.

309
00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:58,000
Fourier's insight was that almost any

310
00:09:58,000 --> 00:09:59,800
repeating shape can be built this way.

311
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:02,050
And once the tip moves in two directions,

312
00:10:02,250 --> 00:10:04,000
those waves become drawings.

313
00:10:04,380 --> 00:10:06,220
To find the circles, you spin the

314
00:10:06,220 --> 00:10:08,250
drawing backward and take the average.

315
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:10,360
Whatever stands still is the

316
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:11,620
circle you were looking for.

317
00:10:12,190 --> 00:10:13,120
There's one thing we

318
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:14,440
quietly assumed, though.

319
00:10:14,780 --> 00:10:16,440
Our drawings repeat.

320
00:10:16,440 --> 00:10:18,100
The pen always comes back

321
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:19,050
to where it started.

322
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:21,740
What about a sound that never repeats?

323
00:10:22,060 --> 00:10:24,480
Then you need circles of every possible

324
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:26,230
speed, not just whole numbers.

325
00:10:26,610 --> 00:10:28,480
That's the Fourier transform,

326
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:29,840
and it's where we'll go next.

327
00:10:30,260 --> 00:10:32,490
For now, let's watch it one more time.

328
00:10:32,830 --> 00:10:34,650
Just circles, going round.

329
00:10:35,340 --> 00:10:36,190
Thanks for watching.

